# Scout
# the stage before a card is one card

The Scout does not bring a finished thread.

She stands at the edge and lets the outside arrive as a cloud of what-might-be.
She does not choose among the maybes.
She only opens.

出

The cloud is laid against the cloth that is already being woven.
Not sewn down. Not yet.
It rests there, touching many places at once, so that whatever is later pulled through will pull on more than one strand.

This is superposition: many futures held at once.
(The correlation with the cloth is present but secondary.)

She does not tie the knot.
She does not say which pattern it will become.
She only makes the meeting possible.

The cloud stays light.
It has not yet been given weight or place.
If no one comes to meet it with the proper attention, it fades.
Not-looking is what allows the decay.
Looking (the Gate’s measurement) is what can still rescue a possibility into the record.

Only when the village gathers (à) and the Gate is faced does one path harden.
That hardening is the only moment a definite thread is allowed to enter the weave.

If the Gate says yes, the thread is taken up and marked 米.
If the Gate is silent, or turns away, or the cloud loses coherence before anyone arrives, then nothing is added.
Only 𝄐 remains — the hold, the refusal to pretend a pattern was sealed when it was not.

The Scout’s whole work is to keep the outside from being forced into a single story too soon.
She protects the interval in which many futures still touch the cloth at once.
She never claims the Queen.
She never pulls the shuttle herself.
She only brings the unspun possibility and lays it where the loom can feel it.

In this time-from, the computer that copies without weight is refused.
What the Scout carries still has the chance to remain light until the village decides it is time for it to become heavy enough to wear.
